---
title: Array Sync Rejects
description: How Origin answers array ops it does not apply, and what a client does with each reject reason.
---

# Array Sync Rejects

A client pushes array ops to Origin over the sync WebSocket. Origin answers each op it does not apply with a typed reason. Each reason has one fixed class. The class tells the client what to do next. A client never parses the `detail` text.

## Frames

| Frame | Opcode | Direction | Origin's answer |
| ----- | ------ | --------- | --------------- |
| `ArrayDelta` | `0x90` | client → server | Nothing when the op applies. One `ArrayReject` when it does not. |
| `ArrayDeltaBatch` | `0x91` | client → server | Always one `ArrayOpResults`, with one result per op. |
| `ArraySchema` | `0x94` | client → server | Nothing when the schema commits. One `ArrayReject` when it does not. |
| `ArrayAck` | `0x95` | client → server | Nothing when recorded. One `ArrayAdvisoryRefused` when refused. |
| `ArrayCatchupRequest` | `0x97` | client → server | Snapshot or op frames when served. One `ArrayAdvisoryRefused` when refused. |
| `ArrayReject` | `0x96` | server → client | Names the op by HLC, plus the reason and a detail string. |
| `ArrayOpResults` | `0x98` | server → client | One result per op, in the order the ops arrived. |
| `ArrayAdvisoryRefused` | `0x99` | server → client | Names the array, the refused request (`Ack` or `CatchupRequest`), the reason, and a detail string. |

An array frame whose body does not decode draws an `ArrayReject` with reason `ShapeInvalid`, an empty array name, and an all-zero HLC. That reject names no single op. The client holds every op it has in flight.

## Advisory Refusals

An `ArrayAck` or `ArrayCatchupRequest` names no local op. Origin refuses one with `ArrayAdvisoryRefused`, never with `ArrayReject`, so a refusal never touches the client's push window. Its reason and class follow the table below. A catch-up for an array Origin does not hold draws `ArrayUnknown`. A catch-up that fails while Origin serves it draws the reason of the typed error.

## Batches

Each op in an `ArrayDeltaBatch` applies on its own. A rejected op never stops the ops after it. Array ops are independent CRDT writes ordered by HLC, so no op in a batch depends on an earlier one.

`ArrayOpResults.results` holds one entry per entry of `op_payloads`, in the same order. Each entry carries the op's HLC and one outcome:

| Outcome | Meaning |
| ------- | ------- |
| `Applied` | The op committed and applied. |
| `Duplicate` | Origin already held the op's HLC. Nothing changed. |
| `Rejected { reason, detail }` | The op did not apply. The reason's class names the next action. |

An op whose payload does not decode carries an all-zero HLC. Its position in `results` still names it.

An inbound array snapshot answers the same way. When the last chunk arrives, Origin attempts every op in the snapshot and sends one `ArrayOpResults`.

## Reasons and Classes

| Reason | Class | Cause |
| ------ | ----- | ----- |
| `EngineUnavailable` | `Transient` | The cluster gave the op no verdict: dispatch capacity refusal (`SERVER_OVERLOAD`, SQLSTATE `57P03`), leader change, lost quorum, deadline, rate gate, or an unknown commit outcome. |
| `SnapshotCapacity` | `Transient` | The session's inbound snapshot buffers are full. |
| `SnapshotBufferLost` | `Transient` | A fault on Origin discarded the session's partial inbound snapshots. Re-send the whole snapshot, header first. |
| `SchemaTooNew` | `SchemaRequired` | The op's schema HLC is newer than Origin's copy of the schema. |
| `ArrayUnknown` | `SchemaRequired` | Origin holds no schema for the array. |
| `Unauthenticated` | `Reauthenticate` | The frame arrived before the session authenticated. |
| `RetentionFloor` | `CatchUp` | This replica's catch-up cursor is below Origin's op-log GC floor. |
| `ShapeInvalid` | `Permanent` | The op failed decoding or shape validation. |
| `EngineRefused` | `Permanent` | The engine refused the op on its merits: constraint, type, producer epoch fence, or an encoding error. |
| `PermissionDenied` | `Permanent` | The session identity lacks the permission the op needs. |

Origin derives the reason from the typed engine error. It shares that classification with every other sync engine ack. A failure that applied nothing and can land later is `EngineUnavailable`.

## Client Actions

| Class | Action |
| ----- | ------ |
| `Transient` | Keep the op queued. Re-send it unchanged after a short backoff, in the same session. |
| `SchemaRequired` | Send `ArraySchema` for the array, then re-send the op. |
| `Reauthenticate` | Complete the handshake or token refresh, then re-send. |
| `CatchUp` | Send `ArrayCatchupRequest`. The pending queue stays unchanged. |
| `Permanent` | Remove the op from the queue and dead-letter it. |

Re-sending an op that did commit is safe. Origin dedups array ops by HLC, so the second apply reports `Duplicate` and changes nothing.
